# TLS for the whole stack.
#
# Caddy is the only thing published to the internet. It obtains and renews a
# Let's Encrypt certificate for each of the five hostnames on its own, the
# first time each one is requested, and hands the request to that app's
# nginx over the compose network. Nothing here needs editing: the hostnames
# come from the same URLs in .env that the API uses to build the links it
# texts and emails, so the certificate and the links cannot disagree.
#
# For certificates to be issued, before the first `docker compose up`:
#   - each hostname's DNS A (and AAAA, if you have IPv6) record points here.
#     That is five: the portal, the console, the admin console, the bare
#     domain and www;
#   - ports 80 and 443 reach this host. Port 80 is not optional: Let's
#     Encrypt proves you own the name over it, and Caddy uses it to redirect
#     plain-HTTP visitors to HTTPS.
#
# Certificates live in the caddy-data volume. Deleting that volume means
# asking Let's Encrypt again, and it rate-limits repeat requests for the same
# names (5 a week), so do not make a habit of it.
#
# Headers: Caddy's reverse_proxy sets X-Forwarded-For, -Proto and -Host by
# default and discards any a client sent. nginx appends its own hop, which is
# why the API is told TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=2 in docker-compose.yml.
{
	# Where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings if renewal ever stops working.
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

(app) {
	encode zstd gzip
	# Photo uploads of up to 50MB; nginx enforces the same ceiling behind it.
	request_body {
		max_size 50MB
	}
}

{$FAMILY_PORTAL_URL} {
	import app
	reverse_proxy family-portal:80 {
		# The photo pack is a zip of hundreds of decrypted images, streamed.
		flush_interval -1
		transport http {
			read_timeout 600s
			write_timeout 600s
		}
	}
}

{$CONSOLE_URL} {
	import app
	reverse_proxy director-console:80 {
		flush_interval -1
		transport http {
			read_timeout 600s
			write_timeout 600s
		}
	}
}

{$ADMIN_CONSOLE_URL} {
	import app
	reverse_proxy admin-console:80
}

# The bare domain, and www.
#
# Both redirect to the console rather than serving anything of their own, and
# that is the whole design: the console's sign-in is the one screen that can
# deal with whoever turns up. Staff sign in there. A platform admin is passed
# on to their own console the moment their password is accepted. A family is
# pointed at the portal, because their texted link is the credential and no
# password would ever have worked for them.
#
# A fourth app here would be a landing page with a sign-in box on it, which is
# the console's sign-in screen at a different hostname plus a second copy of
# everything that has to stay right about it.
#
# 308 rather than 302 so the method and body survive, and a form posted at the
# bare domain is not silently turned into a GET.
#
# ROOT_URL and WWW_URL are required, like the other three, and deliberately not
# made optional: an empty site address here is not something to guess about, and
# a stack whose bare domain quietly became a catch-all would send the family
# portal's traffic to the console. If you do not want the bare domain served,
# delete these two blocks and drop the two variables — a visible edit to this
# file, which is how everything else reachable from the internet is decided.
#
# Each needs its own DNS record and gets its own certificate, so both count
# against Let's Encrypt's five-a-week for these names.
{$ROOT_URL} {
	redir {$CONSOLE_URL}{uri} 308
}

{$WWW_URL} {
	redir {$CONSOLE_URL}{uri} 308
}

# The public website: optional, like the `website` service in
# docker-compose.yml. With WEBSITE_URL unset this block falls back to a plain
# HTTP name under .invalid, which no DNS will ever resolve, so Caddy asks
# Let's Encrypt for nothing and the block is inert. Set WEBSITE_URL and start
# the service with `--profile website` to turn it on.
{$WEBSITE_URL:http://website.invalid} {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy website:80
}
